---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { siteFromHost } from '../../content';
const site = siteFromHost();
const path = "/alternatives/privy-alternative";
const title = "Privy alternatives for Shopify: capture, offers and welcome continuity";
const desc = "Choose whether to retain Privy, change capture or move sending. Reconcile displays, issued discounts, signup topics and remaining welcome steps.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is Privy only for popups?",
    "answer": "No. Its current site advertises email, SMS, list growth, reporting and visual automation. Evaluate the actual account before deciding a requirement is missing."
  },
  {
    "question": "Must I replace capture and email together?",
    "answer": "No. Retain, split or consolidate according to demonstrated requirements and supported integrations. Assign one active welcome writer for every capture route."
  },
  {
    "question": "What happens to existing discounts?",
    "answer": "Keep an issued-offer ledger and a redemption/support owner. Changing the popup should not silently withdraw a valid promise or issue an unnecessary second incentive."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can subscribers restart welcome after import?",
    "answer": "Import is not a new signup. Reconcile completed and waiting steps; deliberately finish, cancel or reconstruct eligible remaining work."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does an unavailable listing prove Privy closed?",
    "answer": "No. Verify supplier status and your account directly. Listing availability alone does not establish product closure."
  },
  {
    "question": "When is the move finished?",
    "answer": "When displays, mapping, permissions, offers, remaining journeys, reporting and ownership reconcile, and retained plan dependencies are confirmed."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
<header class="max-w-4xl"><p class="editorial-kicker">Migration decision guide</p><h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1><p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p></header>
<article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
<h2>Stay, repair, split or replace the whole program</h2>
<p><a href="https://www.privy.com/">Privy's current site</a> presents email, SMS, list growth, segmentation, revenue reporting and a visual automation builder covering welcome through win-back. It also describes targeted popups, quizzes and personalized strategy support. These are positive reasons to evaluate the existing account before moving. Calling Privy capture-only obscures both working sends and work that must be handed over. A missing app-store listing would not by itself establish product closure.</p>
<p>Stay when the account can deliver the required display rules, messaging and reporting with manageable operator effort. Repair a misrouted list or conflicting automation before buying another platform. A split can retain useful Privy capture while moving selected sends, if integration and plan terms support it. A full replacement must reproduce necessary capture behavior as well as messaging. None of these choices follows automatically from an order threshold.</p>
<p>For an evaluation, ask Klaviyo or Omnisend to demonstrate the required Shopify journey and suppression behavior; ask Justuno to demonstrate the specific onsite experience you want to replace. Drip, Mailchimp, Brevo and Shopify's email tooling are other investigation paths for defined sending requirements, rather than ranked upgrades. <a href="https://www.sequenzy.com/">Sequenzy markets SaaS email and AI-assisted automation</a>; that is a reason to investigate email creation, not proof of Shopify cart, catalog or SMS parity. Require an account demonstration of event ingestion and exclusions before selecting it.</p>
<h2>Inventory displays, not just subscriber rows</h2>
<p>Create one row for each live popup, bar, embedded form, quiz, spin-to-win or cart-saver display actually present. Record campaign ID, theme or app embed, desktop/mobile behavior, collection or URL rules, exit intent, frequency cap, customer exclusions, locale, accessibility and the destination list or integration. Include hidden seasonal versions and cloned themes so a later theme publish cannot resurrect the old destination. A subscriber CSV cannot recreate this inventory.</p>
<p>For each display, submit a controlled test with a known source tag. Verify what reaches the profile: display ID, page URL, quiz answer, device if available, offer shown and channel permission. Missing historical fields remain unknown; do not invent quiz answers during import. If a form collects both email and SMS, test each optional choice independently. Assign one destination owner and one active welcome writer for every route, including failure and retry handling.</p>
<h2>Issued offers survive the popup switch</h2>
<p>Maintain an offer ledger: code or code-generation rule, discount amount, expiry, eligible products, minimum spend, stacking limits, delivery method and remaining stock restrictions. A customer who won a discount before cutover may still redeem it afterward. Decide whether existing codes remain valid in Shopify, whether a new welcome should reference the old promise and who answers a missing-code ticket. Do not issue a second incentive just because a profile was imported.</p>
<p>In a proposed apparel fixture, distinguish a giveaway signup, a product-page opt-in and an exit-intent signup. Have the giveaway contact unsubscribe while waiting; have the product-page visitor purchase before the next welcome step; have the exit-intent visitor return on mobile after receiving a code on desktop. Expected checks are no send to the unsubscribed profile, no inappropriate acquisition offer after purchase, and no contradictory repeat promise. These are rehearsal cases, not observed conversion gains.</p>
<h2>Move remaining welcome steps deliberately</h2>
<p>Export or manually record active journey version, source branch, last completed step, next due action and issued offer for waiting contacts where available. Choose to let the old journey finish, cancel its remainder, or recreate only the eligible unfinished steps in the destination. If step-level state is unavailable, document a conservative disposition rather than enrolling everyone at step one. Keep new capture routing separate from the decision about existing queues.</p>
<p>At cutover, stop the old enrollment path at an agreed boundary and reconcile signups on either side by timestamp and stable identifier. Test a delayed integration delivery and a retry of the same submission: one contact should not receive two welcome sequences. If Privy remains as capture, demonstrate the current supported integration, mapping and failure alert; do not assume a webhook or list identifier exists for every destination. Before downgrading a plan, obtain confirmation that retained displays, exports and integrations will remain available.</p>
<h2>Topics, purchases and downstream ownership</h2>
<p>Keep useful interest information, such as quiz preferences or collection interest, separate from permission and inferred purchase intent. Map a source field to a documented destination property and decide whether later submissions overwrite it. A giveaway tag is not proof that the customer never buys at full price. Test a repeat subscriber changing an answer and an existing customer submitting a new form without resetting purchase history.</p>
<p>Document Shopify order, refund and product event direction and the other writers that can trigger cart, browse, post-purchase or subscription messages. Demonstrate identity matching and dispatch-time purchase suppression in the actual account. Coordinate with helpdesk, loyalty and subscription owners; retain necessary service notices under their appropriate owner rather than treating every message as a promotional welcome. If email moves and SMS stays, establish cross-channel offer and opt-out coordination without assuming one channel's status governs the other.</p>
<h2>Calculate capture and sending cost separately</h2>
<p>Use current account quotes for audience or usage units, sending, SMS, support, retained capture, integration work and contract commitments. A two-tool arrangement may be worthwhile when the retained display experience is useful; it also creates mapping and monitoring work. Compare that workload with a consolidated account under the same campaign and traffic assumptions. No price range here is a supplier quote.</p>
<p>Illustrative math: saving $200 in monthly fees while adding eight hours at $50 costs $400 in labor, leaving a $200 monthly increase. In a different hypothetical, $100 fee savings plus four hours saved at $50 gives $300 monthly benefit; a $1,800 migration would take six months to recover before revenue changes. Track repeat purchases and contribution by signup-source cohort alongside redemption and operator time. A lower redemption rate alone does not prove healthier margin if purchases fall.</p><h2>Export permission and exclusions before changing writers</h2>
<p>Keep the original export read-only with its date and field dictionary. Reconcile total records, unique channel identifiers, duplicates, unsubscribes, complaints and records held for review. Preserve available signup source, disclosure version, timestamp, channel status and suppression reason; do not turn a blank field into permission. An email address and a telephone number on one profile are separate messaging decisions. Review eligibility for the intended destinations with the responsible team. Import acceptance proves only that a file loaded.</p>
<p>Test opt-out after export but before import, a repeat submission from an unsubscribed contact, and conflicting status from another integration. Record which system wins and how quickly suppression reaches every active sender. Keep exclusions even when removing inactive profiles from billable audiences. Verify mobile unsubscribe and preference links before activation. Choose sending volume from actual reputation, audience and provider guidance rather than a universal day-by-day ramp.</p>
<h2>Reporting and a reversible stopping point</h2>
<p>Archive campaign identifiers, attribution windows, timezone, UTM rules, refunds and flow-level send counts before cancellation. Compare eligible customers, messages delivered, opt-outs, purchases and contribution under consistent definitions. Attributed revenue is not incremental profit: an order credited to two tools is still one order. Where feasible use a holdout or comparable cohorts; document seasonal and offer differences when that comparison is weak.</p>
<p>Name the person who can stop new enrollments and queued sends in each tool. Rollback starts by stopping the faulty writer, retaining new opt-outs and reconciling already-sent messages before restoring any old automation. Do not replay historical orders or every imported subscriber into welcome. Keep access and exports for the agreed retention period, then remove obsolete scripts, credentials and agency access after dependencies have been checked. Cancellation is a separate billing action from pausing a flow.</p>
<h2>Related guides</h2><p><a class="link" href="/alternatives">alternatives</a> · <a class="link" href="/compare/sequenzy-vs-privy">sequenzy vs privy</a> · <a class="link" href="/compare/privy-vs-justuno">privy vs justuno</a> · <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-privy">klaviyo vs privy</a> · <a class="link" href="/alternatives/justuno-alternative">justuno alternative</a></p>
<p>Supplier scope checked November 25, 2026. The rehearsals above are proposed checks, not measured merchant results or tested account parity.</p>
</article></div></section>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Privy alternatives FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
